## Well-Structured Unit Test Class

```java
class OrderServiceTest {

    // Dependencies — mocked
    @Mock
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Mock
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    @Mock
    private EmailService emailService;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderService orderService;  // Class under test

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this);  // Or use @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("createOrder")
    class CreateOrder {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should create order and send confirmation email")
        void shouldCreateOrderAndSendEmail() {
            // ARRANGE
            CreateOrderRequest request = buildValidRequest();
            Order savedOrder = buildSavedOrder(1L);
            when(orderRepo.save(any(Order.class))).thenReturn(savedOrder);

            // ACT
            Order result = orderService.createOrder(request);

            // ASSERT
            assertThat(result.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
            assertThat(result.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
            verify(emailService).sendOrderConfirmation(savedOrder);
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should throw exception when items list is empty")
        void shouldThrowWhenItemsEmpty() {
            CreateOrderRequest request = new CreateOrderRequest();
            request.setItems(Collections.emptyList());

            assertThatThrownBy(() -> orderService.createOrder(request))
                .isInstanceOf(InvalidOrderException.class)
                .hasMessageContaining("items");
        }
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("cancelOrder")
    class CancelOrder {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should cancel a pending order")
        void shouldCancelPendingOrder() {
            Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(OrderStatus.PENDING);
            when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

            orderService.cancelOrder(1L);

            assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
            verify(orderRepo).save(order);
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should throw when trying to cancel a shipped order")
        void shouldThrowWhenCancellingShippedOrder() {
            Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);
            when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

            assertThatThrownBy(() -> orderService.cancelOrder(1L))
                .isInstanceOf(InvalidOrderStateException.class);
        }
    }

    // Parameterized test for different inputs:
    @ParameterizedTest(name = "status {0} should be cancelable: {1}")
    @CsvSource({
        "PENDING, true",
        "PROCESSING, true",
        "SHIPPED, false",
        "DELIVERED, false",
        "CANCELLED, false"
    })
    void cancelability(OrderStatus status, boolean expectedCancelable) {
        Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(status);
        assertThat(orderService.canBeCancelled(order)).isEqualTo(expectedCancelable);
    }

    // Test data builders:
    private CreateOrderRequest buildValidRequest() {
        CreateOrderRequest req = new CreateOrderRequest();
        req.setUserId(42L);
        req.setItems(List.of(new OrderItemRequest(1L, 2)));
        return req;
    }
}
```

## AssertJ — Fluent Assertions

```java
// JUnit built-in (verbose):
assertEquals("PENDING", order.getStatus().name());
assertTrue(order.getItems().size() > 0);
assertNull(order.getShippedAt());

// AssertJ (fluent, readable, better error messages):
assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
assertThat(order.getItems()).isNotEmpty().hasSize(2);
assertThat(order.getShippedAt()).isNull();
assertThat(order.getTotal()).isGreaterThan(BigDecimal.ZERO);
assertThat(result).extracting(Order::getId, Order::getStatus)
    .containsExactly(42L, OrderStatus.PENDING);

// Collection assertions:
assertThat(orders)
    .hasSize(3)
    .extracting(Order::getStatus)
    .containsOnly(OrderStatus.PENDING, OrderStatus.PROCESSING);

// Exception assertions:
assertThatThrownBy(() -> service.findById(-1L))
    .isInstanceOf(InvalidIdException.class)
    .hasMessage("ID must be positive")
    .hasNoCause();

// Soft assertions — collect all failures:
SoftAssertions soft = new SoftAssertions();
soft.assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
soft.assertThat(order.getTotal()).isEqualByComparingTo("99.99");
soft.assertAll();  // Reports all failures together
```

## Test Naming Conventions

```java
// Pattern: should[ExpectedBehavior]When[Condition]
@Test
void shouldThrowOrderNotFoundWhenIdDoesNotExist() { ... }

// Pattern: given[Context]_when[Action]_then[Outcome]
@Test
void givenPendingOrder_whenCancelCalled_thenStatusIsCancelled() { ... }

// Pattern: [action][Expected result][Description]
@Test
void createOrder_returnsCreatedOrder_withPendingStatus() { ... }

// With @DisplayName — human readable test names in report:
@Test
@DisplayName("Given an order with SHIPPED status, when cancel is called, should throw InvalidOrderStateException")
void test1() { ... }
```

## Production Reality

Aim for tests that are: Fast (milliseconds — no DB, no network), Isolated (no shared state between tests), Repeatable (same result every run), Self-validating (pass/fail clearly determined), and Timely (written alongside or before the code — FIRST acronym). A test suite that's slow or flaky gets skipped. The value of tests comes from running them constantly.

## What to Test in a Service Method

```java
// Service method to test:
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req, String username) {
    // 1. Validate user exists
    User user = userRepo.findByUsername(username)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(username));

    // 2. Check user is not suspended
    if (user.isSuspended()) {
        throw new UserSuspendedException(username);
    }

    // 3. Check item stock
    for (OrderItemRequest item : req.getItems()) {
        Product product = productRepo.findById(item.getProductId())
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(item.getProductId()));
        if (product.getStock() < item.getQuantity()) {
            throw new InsufficientStockException(product.getId(), item.getQuantity(), product.getStock());
        }
    }

    // 4. Calculate total and apply promo
    BigDecimal total = calculateTotal(req.getItems());
    if (req.getPromoCode() != null) {
        total = promoService.apply(req.getPromoCode(), total);
    }

    // 5. Save and notify
    Order order = new Order(user, req.getItems(), total);
    Order saved = orderRepo.save(order);
    eventPublisher.publishEvent(new OrderCreatedEvent(saved));
    return saved;
}

// Test cases needed:
// Happy path: all valid → order created, event published
// Error: user not found → UserNotFoundException
// Error: user is suspended → UserSuspendedException
// Error: product not found → ProductNotFoundException
// Error: insufficient stock → InsufficientStockException
// Branch: promo code present → promoService called; no promo → not called
// Total 6-7 tests covering all branches
```

## Boundary Condition Testing

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("discountBoundaryProvider")
void shouldApplyCorrectDiscount(BigDecimal orderTotal, BigDecimal expectedDiscount) {
    // Discount tiers: <$50=0%, $50-$100=5%, >$100=10%
    BigDecimal actualDiscount = discountCalculator.calculate(orderTotal);
    assertThat(actualDiscount).isEqualByComparingTo(expectedDiscount);
}

static Stream<Arguments> discountBoundaryProvider() {
    return Stream.of(
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(49.99), BigDecimal.ZERO),      // Below threshold
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(50.00), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Exact boundary
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(50.01), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Just above
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(99.99), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Just below next
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(100.00), BigDecimal.TEN),      // Next boundary
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(200.00), BigDecimal.TEN)       // Well above
    );
}
```

## What NOT to Test

```java
// Don't test: Lombok-generated code
@Data
public class Order {
    private Long id;  // Don't test getId(), setId() — Lombok generates them
}

// Don't test: Simple delegation with no logic
public OrderResponse getOrder(Long id) {
    return orderMapper.toResponse(orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow());
    // The mapping and findById are unit tested elsewhere
    // This thin wrapper doesn't add enough logic to warrant its own test
    // → Cover via integration test
}

// Don't test: Spring @Configuration classes
// → Spring's own tests cover bean creation

// DO test: Business logic regardless of complexity appearance
public OrderStatus determineNextStatus(Order order) {
    // Complex state machine — MUST test all transitions
    return switch (order.getStatus()) {
        case PENDING -> order.isPaymentConfirmed() ? PROCESSING : PENDING;
        case PROCESSING -> order.isShipped() ? SHIPPED : PROCESSING;
        // ...
    };
}
```

## Production Reality

Aim for meaningful test coverage of business logic — typically 80%+ coverage of service/domain classes. Use JaCoCo in CI to track coverage trends, not as an absolute gate. A coverage decrease of >5% in a PR should trigger review. Exclude: generated code (Lombok, MapStruct, QueryDSL), configuration classes, simple DTOs, and exception classes from coverage requirements.

`@SpringBootTest` is appropriate for tests that verify **cross-cutting behavior** — how the full application hangs together.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class ApplicationSmokeTest {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void healthEndpointReturnsUp() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("/actuator/health", String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

`RANDOM_PORT` starts the actual HTTP server — not MockMvc — on a random port. This tests the full HTTP stack including filters, exception handlers, and content negotiation. Use this for smoke tests, security integration tests, and scenarios where MockMvc's simulated request pipeline doesn't catch the issue.

`MOCK` (default) doesn't start a real server but provides a mock servlet environment. Combined with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, this gives you `MockMvc` in a full context — useful when you need all beans loaded but don't need a real HTTP server.

Reserve `@SpringBootTest` for the 10-20% of tests that genuinely need the full context. The other 80% should use slices.

`@Mock` for plain unit tests — the most common case.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {
    @Mock OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Mock PaymentClient paymentClient;
    @InjectMocks OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void shouldCreateOrder() {
        when(orderRepository.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));
        Order result = orderService.create(new OrderRequest("item", 10));
        assertThat(result.getStatus()).isEqualTo(Status.CREATED);
    }
}
```

No Spring context, no startup time. `@InjectMocks` creates the service and injects the mocks through constructor injection.

`@Spy` when you need mostly real behavior with one override.

```java
@Spy OrderValidator validator = new OrderValidator();
doReturn(true).when(validator).checkInventory(anyString());
```

`@MockBean` only in Spring integration tests where the real bean exists in the context and you need to replace it — typically for external service clients that you can't call in tests.

Sometimes the important assertion isn't the return value — it's what your code passed to its dependencies. `ArgumentCaptor` captures the arguments passed to mock methods for detailed assertions.

```java
@Test
void shouldSendWelcomeEmail() {
    ArgumentCaptor<EmailRequest> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(EmailRequest.class);

    userService.register(new RegisterRequest("john@test.com", "John"));

    verify(emailClient).send(captor.capture());
    EmailRequest sent = captor.getValue();
    assertThat(sent.getTo()).isEqualTo("john@test.com");
    assertThat(sent.getTemplate()).isEqualTo("welcome");
}
```

`verify()` confirms the mock method was called. `verify(mock, times(2))` checks it was called exactly twice. `verify(mock, never())` confirms it was never called — useful for testing that error paths don't trigger side effects.

The combination of `ArgumentCaptor` + `verify()` is essential for testing services that coordinate between dependencies: "did the service call the email client with the right template, the right recipient, and the right data?" Without this pattern, you'd need integration tests for what should be unit-testable coordination logic.

The production-ready pattern uses a shared container across all `@DataJpaTest` classes to avoid starting a new database for each test class.

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
@Testcontainers
class OrderRepositoryTest {
    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
            .withDatabaseName("testdb");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldFindOrdersByStatus() {
        orderRepository.save(new Order("ORD-1", Status.PENDING));
        orderRepository.save(new Order("ORD-2", Status.SHIPPED));

        List<Order> pending = orderRepository.findByStatus(Status.PENDING);
        assertThat(pending).hasSize(1);
        assertThat(pending.get(0).getOrderId()).isEqualTo("ORD-1");
    }
}
```

`@DynamicPropertySource` injects the container's JDBC URL, username, and password into Spring's environment — overriding whatever is in application properties. The container starts once per test class (`static` field), and each `@Test` method gets its own transaction that's rolled back after the test (`@DataJpaTest` enables `@Transactional` by default).

For sharing a container across ALL test classes (not just one), define the container in a base class or use Testcontainers' reuse feature with `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`. A single PostgreSQL container takes ~3 seconds to start — starting one per test class multiplies this across dozens of classes.

Repository tests should focus on queries that could break — not trivial CRUD that Spring Data generates correctly.

```java
@Test
void shouldFindOverdueOrdersWithJoinFetch() {
    Customer customer = customerRepository.save(new Customer("John"));
    Order overdue = orderRepository.save(
        new Order(customer, Status.PENDING, LocalDate.now().minusDays(30)));
    Order recent = orderRepository.save(
        new Order(customer, Status.PENDING, LocalDate.now()));

    List<Order> result = orderRepository.findOverdueWithCustomer(LocalDate.now().minusDays(7));

    assertThat(result).containsExactly(overdue);
    assertThat(result.get(0).getCustomer().getName()).isEqualTo("John");
}
```

Test **custom `@Query` methods** (the SQL could be wrong), **derived queries with multiple conditions** (the generated SQL might not match your expectation), and **pagination/sorting** (edge cases with empty results, single pages). Don't test `save()` and `findById()` — Spring Data has its own test suite for those.

Also test **constraint violations**: inserting a duplicate unique key should throw `DataIntegrityViolationException`. This verifies that your JPA annotations (`@Column(unique = true)`) and database constraints are aligned — a mismatch between entity annotations and Flyway migrations is a common bug that only surfaces in integration tests.

A good MockMvc test verifies the full request-response contract: HTTP method, URL, request body, status code, response body, and headers.

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean UserService userService;

    @Test
    void createUser_returnsCreatedWithLocation() throws Exception {
        when(userService.create(any())).thenReturn(new User(1L, "John", "john@test.com"));

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"john@test.com\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(header().exists("Location"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("John"));
    }

    @Test
    void createUser_withInvalidEmail_returns400() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"not-an-email\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("email"));
    }
}
```

The validation test doesn't need the service mock configured — Spring's `@Valid` rejects the request before the controller method executes. This is important: **validation tests verify Spring's behavior, not your service logic.**

`jsonPath()` uses JSONPath expressions to assert on specific fields in the response body. For complex responses, `@JsonTest` with `JacksonTester` is more readable than deeply nested jsonPath assertions.

MockMvc's value extends beyond happy-path testing. Error handling, security, and custom Spring MVC components are where integration tests catch bugs that unit tests miss.

```java
@Test
void shouldReturn404ForUnknownUser() throws Exception {
    when(userService.findById(999L)).thenThrow(new UserNotFoundException(999L));

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/999"))
        .andExpect(status().isNotFound())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.message").value("User 999 not found"));
}

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
void adminCanDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isNoContent());
}

@Test
@WithAnonymousUser
void anonymousCannotDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
}
```

The `@WithMockUser` annotation creates a mock authentication — no real login required. It works with `@WebMvcTest` because Spring Security's auto-configuration is included in the web slice. Test both the allowed and denied paths — a security test that only verifies "admin can access" without verifying "non-admin cannot access" proves nothing.

`@ControllerAdvice` exception handling is tested implicitly: throw the exception from the mocked service, and verify that MockMvc returns the correct status code and error body. This catches `@ExceptionHandler` mapping issues that only surface with a running Spring context.

For file upload testing, MockMvc supports `MockMultipartFile`. For custom `HandlerMethodArgumentResolver` implementations (like resolving the current user from a JWT token), MockMvc tests verify that the resolver integrates correctly with the controller — something a plain unit test can't validate because argument resolution requires the Spring MVC pipeline.

The most effective pattern uses a static container in a base class that all test classes extend.

```java
public abstract class PostgresTestBase {
    static final PostgreSQLContainer<?> POSTGRES =
        new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
            .withDatabaseName("testdb")
            .withReuse(true);

    static {
        POSTGRES.start();
    }

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", POSTGRES::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", POSTGRES::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", POSTGRES::getPassword);
    }
}
```

The `static {}` initializer starts the container when the class loads — once for the entire test run. `withReuse(true)` keeps the container running between test executions (requires `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`), eliminating startup time even across separate `mvn test` invocations during development.

For multi-container setups (PostgreSQL + Redis + Kafka), define all containers in the base class. Docker Compose integration is available via `DockerComposeContainer` for complex environments, but individual containers are simpler to manage and parallelize.

For methods that return `CompletableFuture`, testing is straightforward: call the method, get the result with a timeout.

```java
@Test
void shouldFetchUserAsync() {
    CompletableFuture<User> future = userService.findByIdAsync(1L);

    User result = assertTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5), () -> future.get());

    assertThat(result.getName()).isEqualTo("John");
}

@Test
void shouldHandleAsyncFailure() {
    CompletableFuture<User> future = userService.findByIdAsync(999L);

    assertThatThrownBy(() -> future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
        .hasCauseInstanceOf(UserNotFoundException.class);
}
```

JUnit 5's `assertTimeout()` fails with a clear message if the future doesn't complete in time. For exception testing, `CompletableFuture.get()` wraps the original exception in `ExecutionException` — use `.hasCauseInstanceOf()` to assert on the actual cause.

For `CompletableFuture` chains (`thenApply`, `thenCompose`, `exceptionally`), test the final result, not intermediate stages. The chain's correctness is proven by the output, not by verifying each transformation step.

Methods annotated with `@Async` that return `void` are the hardest to test — there's no future to wait on. The test must observe a side effect (database write, event publish) to know the async work completed.

```java
@SpringBootTest
class NotificationServiceTest {
    @Autowired NotificationService notificationService;
    @Autowired NotificationRepository notificationRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldSendNotificationAsync() {
        notificationService.sendAsync(new NotificationRequest("user@test.com", "Hello"));

        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
               .untilAsserted(() -> {
                   List<Notification> sent = notificationRepository.findByEmail("user@test.com");
                   assertThat(sent).hasSize(1);
                   assertThat(sent.get(0).getMessage()).isEqualTo("Hello");
               });
    }
}
```

Awaitility's `await().atMost().untilAsserted()` polls the assertion repeatedly until it passes or the timeout expires. This adapts to actual execution speed — fast locally, tolerant of CI slowness.

Note: `@Async` requires `@EnableAsync` and a Spring context to work — you can't test it with plain Mockito. The `@SpringBootTest` is necessary because the async proxy is created by Spring's post-processor. Verify that your test configuration includes `@EnableAsync`, otherwise the method runs synchronously and the test passes for the wrong reason.

For testing `CompletableFuture.allOf()` or `anyOf()` compositions, use `assertTimeout()` on the composed future. Test both the success path (all futures complete) and failure path (one future fails and the composition handles it). This verifies that your composition logic propagates exceptions correctly and doesn't silently swallow failures.

```java
@WebMvcTest(AdminController.class)
class AdminControllerSecurityTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean AdminService adminService;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void adminCanAccessDashboard() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void regularUserCannotAccessAdmin() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isForbidden());
    }

    @Test
    @WithAnonymousUser
    void anonymousIsRedirectedToLogin() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/admin/dashboard"))
            .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
    }
}
```

Three tests, three roles, three expected outcomes. The test class verifies the complete security rule. `@WithMockUser` defaults to username "user" and role "USER" if no parameters are specified — useful for endpoints that just require authentication without specific roles.

For JWT-based security or custom `UserDetails` implementations, create a custom annotation that builds the appropriate authentication object. `@WithSecurityContext` lets you define a factory that populates the `SecurityContext` with your custom principal, authorities, and claims.

For testing CSRF protection, MockMvc provides `csrf()` post-processor: `mockMvc.perform(post("/api/orders").with(csrf()))`. Without it, POST/PUT/DELETE requests fail with 403 when CSRF is enabled. Testing CSRF explicitly ensures your security configuration handles both stateless API endpoints (CSRF disabled) and stateful web endpoints (CSRF enabled) correctly.

Controller-level security (`@RequestMapping` + security config) is tested via MockMvc. Method-level security (`@PreAuthorize`, `@Secured` on service methods) requires a Spring context because the security proxy wraps the bean.

```java
@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceSecurityTest {
    @Autowired OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "MANAGER")
    void managerCanApproveOrder() {
        assertDoesNotThrow(() -> orderService.approve(1L));
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "USER")
    void regularUserCannotApprove() {
        assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class, () -> orderService.approve(1L));
    }
}
```

The `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('MANAGER')")` annotation on the service method is enforced by a Spring AOP proxy. Without a Spring context, the proxy doesn't exist and the method executes without authorization checks. This is why method-level security tests require `@SpringBootTest` — the proxy must be active for the security rule to apply.

Always test the **denied** path. An `@PreAuthorize` that's misconfigured (wrong SpEL expression, missing `@EnableMethodSecurity`) silently allows everything. The denied-path test catches this: if `assertThrows(AccessDeniedException.class)` doesn't throw, the security rule isn't active.

For OAuth2 resource servers, use `SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors.jwt()` to create mock JWT tokens: `mockMvc.perform(get("/api/data").with(jwt().authorities(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("SCOPE_read"))))`. This tests that your security rules correctly inspect JWT claims and scopes without needing a running authorization server.

**`@ValueSource`** handles single-argument tests with primitives.

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@ValueSource(strings = {"", " ", "  "})
void shouldRejectBlankUsernames(String username) {
    assertThrows(ValidationException.class, () -> userService.validate(username));
}
```

**`@CsvSource`** handles multi-argument tests with input-output pairs.

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@CsvSource({
    "100, 10, 10.00",     // regular
    "100, 0, 0.00",       // zero discount
    "0, 50, 0.00",        // zero price
    "99.99, 15, 85.00"    // rounding
})
void shouldCalculateDiscount(double price, int percent, double expected) {
    assertThat(calculator.discount(price, percent))
        .isEqualByComparingTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(expected));
}
```

**`@MethodSource`** handles complex objects, custom types, or when you need setup logic.

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("invalidOrderRequests")
void shouldRejectInvalidOrders(OrderRequest request, String expectedError) {
    ValidationResult result = validator.validate(request);
    assertThat(result.getErrors()).contains(expectedError);
}

static Stream<Arguments> invalidOrderRequests() {
    return Stream.of(
        Arguments.of(new OrderRequest(null, 10), "Item name is required"),
        Arguments.of(new OrderRequest("Widget", -1), "Quantity must be positive"),
        Arguments.of(new OrderRequest("Widget", 0), "Quantity must be positive")
    );
}
```

The `@MethodSource` method must be `static` and return `Stream<Arguments>`. Name it descriptively — it appears in test reports. Each `Arguments.of()` row maps to the test method's parameters in order.

The display name defaults to the arguments, but `@ParameterizedTest(name = "{index}: {0} with {1}% discount")` gives readable test reports. Clear names matter because a failed parameterized test must immediately show which input combination failed.

**`@NullSource` and `@EmptySource`** are convenience annotations that add `null` and empty values to your parameter list. `@NullAndEmptySource` combines both. These are especially useful for validation testing: `@ParameterizedTest @NullAndEmptySource @ValueSource(strings = {" ", "  "}) void shouldRejectInvalidInput(String input)` tests null, empty string, and whitespace variants in one method.

For shared parameter sets used across multiple test classes, define a `@MethodSource` provider class with `static` factory methods and reference them with the fully qualified name: `@MethodSource("com.app.TestDataProviders#invalidEmails")`. This centralizes test data and prevents duplication across test classes.

```java
@SpringBootTest
@EmbeddedKafka(partitions = 1, topics = {"order-events"})
class OrderConsumerTest {
    @Autowired KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> template;
    @Autowired OrderRepository repository;

    @Test
    void shouldProcessOrderEvent() {
        template.send("order-events", "ORD-1", new OrderEvent("ORD-1", BigDecimal.TEN));

        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
               .untilAsserted(() -> {
                   assertThat(repository.findByOrderId("ORD-1")).isNotNull();
               });
    }

    @Test
    void shouldRouteToDeadLetterOnInvalidEvent() {
        template.send("order-events", "BAD", new OrderEvent(null, null));

        ConsumerRecord<String, OrderEvent> dlt =
            KafkaTestUtils.getSingleRecord(dltConsumer, "order-events.DLT",
                Duration.ofSeconds(10));
        assertThat(dlt.key()).isEqualTo("BAD");
    }
}
```

`partitions = 1` ensures deterministic ordering — multiple partitions introduce non-deterministic assignment that makes assertions about order unreliable. `KafkaTestUtils.getSingleRecord()` waits for one record on the specified topic, providing clean DLT verification.

For producer tests, use a test consumer to verify the message was sent with the correct key, value, headers, and partition. `KafkaTestUtils.getRecords()` returns all records from a topic, useful for batch verification.

**Red: Write a failing test that specifies the behavior you want.**

```java
@Test
void shouldApplyPercentageDiscount() {
    PriceCalculator calculator = new PriceCalculator();
    Money result = calculator.applyDiscount(Money.of(100), Discount.percentage(20));
    assertThat(result).isEqualTo(Money.of(80));
}
```

This test doesn't compile yet — `PriceCalculator`, `Money`, and `Discount` don't exist. The test drives the API design: a calculator takes a price and a discount, and returns a discounted price. The naming, the types, the behavior — all decided before writing any implementation.

**Green: Write the minimum code to make the test pass.** Create the classes, implement `applyDiscount` with the simplest logic. Don't optimize, don't handle edge cases yet.

```java
public Money applyDiscount(Money price, Discount discount) {
    return Money.of(price.amount().multiply(
        BigDecimal.ONE.subtract(discount.rate())));
}
```

**Refactor: Improve the code without changing behavior.** Extract methods, rename for clarity, apply patterns — the test suite verifies that refactoring doesn't break anything. This is where TDD's safety net pays off.

Then write the next test — the next behavior you want: zero discount, 100% discount, negative price. Each test adds one requirement. The implementation grows incrementally to satisfy each new test.

`verify()` proves that your code called a dependency. Without it, you can only assert on return values — which misses side effects like sending emails, publishing events, or logging audit trails.

```java
@Test
void shouldNotifyOnOrderCreation() {
    when(orderRepo.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));

    orderService.create(new OrderRequest("Widget", 5));

    verify(orderRepo).save(any(Order.class));
    verify(notificationClient).send(any(OrderNotification.class));
    verify(auditLogger, never()).logFailure(any());
}
```

`verify(mock, times(1))` is the default — `verify(mock)` means "called exactly once." `verify(mock, never())` confirms a method was NOT called — essential for testing error paths where certain side effects should not happen. `verify(mock, atLeast(2))` and `verify(mock, atMost(3))` handle variable interaction counts.

After all verifications, `verifyNoMoreInteractions(mock)` confirms no unexpected calls were made. Use this sparingly — it makes tests brittle by coupling to implementation details. Prefer verifying specific important interactions over asserting nothing else happened.

Sometimes knowing a method was called isn't enough — you need to inspect the argument that was passed. `ArgumentCaptor` captures it for detailed assertions.

```java
@Test
void shouldSendCorrectNotification() {
    when(orderRepo.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> {
        Order o = inv.getArgument(0);
        o.setId(42L);
        return o;
    });

    ArgumentCaptor<OrderNotification> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(OrderNotification.class);

    orderService.create(new OrderRequest("Widget", 5));

    verify(notificationClient).send(captor.capture());
    OrderNotification notification = captor.getValue();
    assertThat(notification.getOrderId()).isEqualTo(42L);
    assertThat(notification.getItemName()).isEqualTo("Widget");
    assertThat(notification.getQuantity()).isEqualTo(5);
}
```

The captor replaces `any()` in the `verify()` call. After verification, `captor.getValue()` returns the captured argument. For multiple invocations, `captor.getAllValues()` returns a list of all captured arguments.

This pattern is essential for testing services that transform data between layers — "did the service correctly map the request DTO to the domain object before saving?" Without ArgumentCaptor, you'd need integration tests for what should be unit-testable mapping logic.

`@SpringBootTest` is appropriate for tests that verify **cross-cutting behavior** — how the full application hangs together.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class ApplicationSmokeTest {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void healthEndpointReturnsUp() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("/actuator/health", String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

`RANDOM_PORT` starts the actual HTTP server — not MockMvc — on a random port. This tests the full HTTP stack including filters, exception handlers, and content negotiation. Use this for smoke tests, security integration tests, and scenarios where MockMvc's simulated request pipeline doesn't catch the issue.

`MOCK` (default) doesn't start a real server but provides a mock servlet environment. Combined with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, this gives you `MockMvc` in a full context — useful when you need all beans loaded but don't need a real HTTP server.

Reserve `@SpringBootTest` for the 10-20% of tests that genuinely need the full context. The other 80% should use slices.

The most effective pattern uses a static container in a base class that all test classes extend.

```java
public abstract class PostgresTestBase {
    static final PostgreSQLContainer<?> POSTGRES =
        new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
            .withDatabaseName("testdb")
            .withReuse(true);

    static {
        POSTGRES.start();
    }

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", POSTGRES::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", POSTGRES::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", POSTGRES::getPassword);
    }
}
```

The `static {}` initializer starts the container when the class loads — once for the entire test run. `withReuse(true)` keeps the container running between test executions (requires `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`), eliminating startup time even across separate `mvn test` invocations during development.

For multi-container setups (PostgreSQL + Redis + Kafka), define all containers in the base class. Docker Compose integration is available via `DockerComposeContainer` for complex environments, but individual containers are simpler to manage and parallelize.

A good MockMvc test verifies the full request-response contract: HTTP method, URL, request body, status code, response body, and headers.

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean UserService userService;

    @Test
    void createUser_returnsCreatedWithLocation() throws Exception {
        when(userService.create(any())).thenReturn(new User(1L, "John", "john@test.com"));

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"john@test.com\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(header().exists("Location"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("John"));
    }

    @Test
    void createUser_withInvalidEmail_returns400() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\\"name\\":\\"John\\",\\"email\\":\\"not-an-email\\"}"))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("email"));
    }
}
```

The validation test doesn't need the service mock configured — Spring's `@Valid` rejects the request before the controller method executes. This is important: **validation tests verify Spring's behavior, not your service logic.**

`jsonPath()` uses JSONPath expressions to assert on specific fields in the response body. For complex responses, `@JsonTest` with `JacksonTester` is more readable than deeply nested jsonPath assertions.

MockMvc's value extends beyond happy-path testing. Error handling, security, and custom Spring MVC components are where integration tests catch bugs that unit tests miss.

```java
@Test
void shouldReturn404ForUnknownUser() throws Exception {
    when(userService.findById(999L)).thenThrow(new UserNotFoundException(999L));

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/999"))
        .andExpect(status().isNotFound())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.message").value("User 999 not found"));
}

@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
void adminCanDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isNoContent());
}

@Test
@WithAnonymousUser
void anonymousCannotDeleteUser() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(delete("/api/users/1"))
        .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
}
```

The `@WithMockUser` annotation creates a mock authentication — no real login required. It works with `@WebMvcTest` because Spring Security's auto-configuration is included in the web slice. Test both the allowed and denied paths — a security test that only verifies "admin can access" without verifying "non-admin cannot access" proves nothing.

`@ControllerAdvice` exception handling is tested implicitly: throw the exception from the mocked service, and verify that MockMvc returns the correct status code and error body. This catches `@ExceptionHandler` mapping issues that only surface with a running Spring context.

For file upload testing, MockMvc supports `MockMultipartFile`. For custom `HandlerMethodArgumentResolver` implementations (like resolving the current user from a JWT token), MockMvc tests verify that the resolver integrates correctly with the controller — something a plain unit test can't validate because argument resolution requires the Spring MVC pipeline.

